    {
      "abstract": "In this paper, I respond to Bernd Ladwig’s critique of my politically liberal approach to the treatment of animals. In my account, I argue that rational disagreement about animals’ moral status necessitates a public justification procedure to identify principles acceptable across diverse but epistemically filtered views on animals. The procedure yields a conclusive principle of minimizing animal suffering, while concerning animals’ interests in life and liberty it is inconclusive and such issues should be settled through democratic procedures. Ladwig argues that politics-first approaches like mine are too complacent with the status quo and anthropocentric because they do not take animals’ interests directly into account. In response, I clarify that while only humans participate in the public justification procedure, the procedure secures animal interests by requiring that principles be acceptable to all reasonable human views, including those that prioritize animal welfare. In conclusion, I reject the idea endorsed by Ladwig—that only a morality-first approach can criticize the status quo—​and defend the view that political liberalism can extend beyond constitutional essentials to meaningfully address animal interests without imposing controversial moral views.",
