    {
      "abstract": "Abstract This article considers the reasons for establishing judicial power and for structuring and exercising it reasonably. Natural law calls for institutions with authority to make and change law, and with authority to resolve disputes about this positive law. The ruler should found courts and differentiate judicial power, making provision for fair adjudication and for the rule of law. In adjudicating disputes, courts will change the legal position of the parties. Some judgments may change the general law, but there is good reason for courts to eschew general lawmaking responsibility. Courts should interpret enactments consistently with the intention of the lawmaker, a responsibility that is compatible with equitable interpretation. Natural law bears on how courts should be structured and the conditions under which judicial power should be exercised. Natural law does not confer on courts a general jurisdiction to adjudicate disputes about the constitution or to invalidate unjust laws. While natural law affirms human rights, it does not require human rights law, which may implicate courts in making law that threatens the common good.",
