      "abstract": "Abstract Intentionalism holds that the referent of a demonstrative is determined, at least in part, by the speaker’s referential intention. Although the view leaves open what kind of intention is required, it is natural to assume that successfully referring to a particular object requires intending to refer to that very object. This paper challenges this assumption and develops a weaker intentionalist account that permits demonstrative reference without a single target fixed in advance. Drawing on considerations from action theory, particularly concerning the Simple View, I introduce cases in which speakers appear to secure a referent despite being open to—but not unsettled about—multiple courses of action. Next, I address objections before considering a potential solution based on Jeffrey King’s analysis of felicitous underspecification. Finally, I propose an alternative account in terms of partial intentions and discuss its relation to the broader perspective that weaker referential pro-attitudes may suffice for demonstrative reference.",
