    {
      "abstract": "This article undertakes a close textual analysis of five widely circulated Indian femvertising campaigns produced between 2016 and 2024: Ariel India's ‘Dads #ShareTheLoad’, Gillette India's ‘Man Enough’, Tanishq's ‘Ekatvam’, Brooke Bond Red Label's ‘Swad Apnepan Ka’ films and Dove India's ‘#StopTheBeautyTest’. I argue that Indian femvertising enacts a double erasure: it neutralizes structural feminist critique through neoliberal incorporation while rendering caste and its intersection with gender categorically invisible. I address that gap by proposing the concept of casteist postfeminism to describe the specific discursive operation of Indian femvertising. An intersectional Dalit feminist advertising criticism that is adequate to its object must therefore be reflexive about its own conditions of production, attending to the ways in which academic feminist scholarship has reproduced the upper-caste centring it critiques in popular culture.",
